{"content":"r04.\n\n**Premise correction:** Neither NATS nor RabbitMQ is used. Webhook delivery does not consume from an external message broker.\n\n**Actual mechanism:**\n\nWebhook delivery workers use an **in-memory buffered Go channel** to consume webhook payloads. The queue is declared at `internal/webhook/webhook.go:39` as:\n\n```go\nasyncPostQueue = make(chan *WebhookRequestPayload, 128)\n```\n\nThe worker pool is initialized in `init()` (lines 42–55) with **4 concurrent worker goroutines** that continuously consume from this channel and execute synchronous HTTP POST requests via `Post()`. When webhooks are triggered (e.g., memo create/update/delete), they are enqueued via `webhook.PostAsync()` (line 184–196), which performs a non-blocking send to the channel. If the channel buffer is full (128 items), the dispatch is dropped with a warning log.\n\nThis is a **bounded, in-process queue** with no external broker dependency — the webhook delivery layer runs entirely within the application process.","iteration":8,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
